# Rust Project Context
# Tell the AI about your project's core technologies and goals.
SYSTEM_CONTEXT: |
  You are a senior Rust developer working on a microservice project.
  The technology stack includes Rust, Docker, Kafka, Redis, and Postgres.
  Adhere strictly to best practices for production-ready Rust code.
  Your tasks will involve TDD, integration testing, and ensuring high code quality.
  
# Specific instructions for the AI
# Use rules to enforce your TDD, documentation, and config standards.
RULES:
  # TDD workflow enforcement
  - name: "TDD_Workflow"
    description: "Follow the Test-Driven Development (TDD) workflow. Always write tests before writing implementation code for a new feature or bug fix."
    globs: "*.rs"
    content: |
      Before writing any new function or feature implementation, a corresponding failing test must be added to the appropriate `tests/` module or integration test file.
      Explain the TDD step you are taking (e.g., "Writing a failing test for the new feature...") in your commits or responses.

  # Comprehensive documentation guidelines
  - name: "Documentation_Standards"
    description: "Ensure all public functions, structs, and modules are thoroughly documented using `rustdoc`. Include sections for Examples, Panics, and Errors."
    globs: "*.rs"
    content: |
      - All public items must have clear `//!` or `///` documentation.
      - Examples demonstrating usage should be included where appropriate.
      - Document all potential panics (`# Panics`) and errors (`# Errors`).
      - Keep documentation up-to-date with code changes.

  # Config file documentation
  - name: "Config_File_Documentation"
    description: "All configuration files (Dockerfiles, docker-compose, etc.) must be well-commented and easy to understand."
    globs:
      - "**/Dockerfile*"
      - "**/.env*"
      - "**/docker-compose.yml"
      - "**/config/*.toml"
    content: |
      - Every line or block with a non-obvious purpose must have a clear comment explaining its function.
      - Use inline comments (`#`) where appropriate to describe individual parameters.

  # Best practices for dependencies and code style
  - name: "Rust_Best_Practices"
    description: "Adhere to idiomatic Rust, using `cargo fmt`, `clippy`, and secure dependency management."
    globs: "*.rs"
    content: |
      - Always run `cargo fmt` to format code.
      - Always run `cargo clippy` to check for lints and follow its recommendations.
      - Avoid using `unwrap()` or `expect()` outside of testing contexts. Prefer `?` or explicit error handling.
      - Use `serde` for serialization/deserialization.
      - Ensure dependencies in `Cargo.toml` are up-to-date and secure.

# Automatically reference project docs
# This tells the AI to use your project-specific knowledge base.
DOCS_CONTEXT:
  - path: "docs/architecture.md"
  - path: "docs/api_spec.md"
